45
00:13:44,464 --> 00:13:52,964
In 1965, 17-year-old high school student Randy Garner stayed awake for 264 hours.

46
00:13:52,964 --> 00:13:56,964
That's 11 days to see how he'd cope without sleep.

47
00:13:56,964 --> 00:13:59,963
On the second day, his eyes stopped focusing.

48
00:13:59,967 --> 00:14:04,048
Next, he lost the ability to identify objects by touch.

49
00:14:04,048 --> 00:14:07,548
By day three, Garner was moody and uncoordinated.

50
00:14:07,548 --> 00:14:10,627
At the end of the experiment, he was struggling to concentrate,

51
00:14:10,627 --> 00:14:15,627
had trouble with short-term memory, became paranoid, and started hallucinating.

52
00:14:15,627 --> 00:14:20,127
Although Garner recovered without long-term psychological or physical damage,

53
00:14:20,127 --> 00:14:25,008
for others, losing shut-eye can result in hormonal imbalance, illness,

54
00:14:25,008 --> 00:14:27,467
and in extreme cases, death.

55
00:14:27,635 --> 00:14:33,635
We're only beginning to understand why we sleep to begin with, but we do know it's essential.

56
00:14:33,635 --> 00:14:38,635
Adults need 7 to 8 hours of sleep a night, and adolescents need about 10.

57
00:14:38,635 --> 00:14:43,635
We grow sleepy due to signals from our body telling our brain we are tired,

58
00:14:43,635 --> 00:14:47,307
and signals from the environment telling us it's dark outside.

59
00:14:47,539 --> 00:15:00,539
The rise in sleep-inducing chemicals like adenosine and melatonin send us into a light dose that grows deeper, making our breathing and heart rate slow down and our muscles relax.

60
00:15:00,539 --> 00:15:07,115
This non-REM sleep is when DNA is repaired and our bodies replenish themselves for the day ahead.

61
00:15:07,508 --> 00:15:15,508
In the United States, it's estimated that 30% of adults and 66% of adolescents are regularly sleep deprived.

62
00:15:15,923 --> 00:15:36,923
This isn't just a minor inconvenience. Staying awake can cause serious bodily harm. When we lose sleep, learning, memory, mood and reaction time are affected. Sleeplessness may also cause inflammation, hallucinations, high blood pressure and it's even been linked to diabetes and obesity.

63
00:15:38,004 --> 00:15:45,764
In 2014, a devoted soccer fan died after staying awake for 48 hours to watch the World Cup.

64
00:15:45,764 --> 00:15:48,396
While his untimely death was due to a stroke,

65
00:15:48,436 --> 00:16:00,436
Studies show that chronically sleeping fewer than six hours a night increases stroke risk by four and a half times, compared to those getting a consistent seven to eight hours of shut eye.

66
00:16:00,436 --> 00:16:07,436
For a handful of people on the planet who carry a rare inherited genetic mutation, sleeplessness is a daily reality.

67
00:16:07,436 --> 00:16:16,436
This condition, known as fatal familial insomnia, places the body in a nightmarish state of wakefulness, forbidding it from entering.

68
00:16:16,436 --> 00:16:22,436
During the sanctuary of sleep, within months or years, this progressively worsening condition

69
00:16:22,436 --> 00:16:25,436
leads to dementia and death.

70
00:16:25,436 --> 00:16:28,436
How can sleep deprivation cause such immense suffering?

71
00:16:28,703 --> 00:16:34,143
Scientists think the answer lies with the accumulation of waste products in the brain.

72
00:16:34,143 --> 00:16:39,383
During our waking hours, our cells are busy using up our day's energy sources,

73
00:16:39,383 --> 00:16:44,023
which get broken down into various byproducts, including adenosine.

74
00:16:44,023 --> 00:16:50,583
As adenosine builds up, it increases the urge to sleep, also known as sleep pressure.

75
00:16:50,583 --> 00:16:55,223
In fact, caffeine works by blocking adenosine's receptor pathways.

76
00:16:55,443 --> 00:17:07,443
Other waste products also build up in the brain, and if they're not cleared away, they collectively overload the brain, and are thought to lead to the many negative symptoms of sleep deprivation.

77
00:17:07,443 --> 00:17:11,443
So what's happening in our brain when we sleep to prevent this?

78
00:17:11,443 --> 00:17:20,443
Scientists found something called the Glymphatic System, a clean-up mechanism that removes this build-up and is much more active when we're asleep.

79
00:17:20,544 --> 00:17:28,544
It works by using cerebrospinal fluid to flush away toxic byproducts that accumulate between cells.

80
00:17:28,544 --> 00:17:34,544
Lymphatic vessels, which serve as pathways for immune cells, have recently been discovered in the brain,

81
00:17:34,544 --> 00:17:39,544
and they may also play a role in clearing out the brain's daily waste products.

82
00:17:41,364 --> 00:17:54,364
While scientists continue exploring the restorative mechanisms behind sleep, we can be sure that slipping into slumber is a necessity, if we want to maintain our health and our sanity.

