but believe it or not, you might be better off closing the books, putting away the music, and going to sleep.

290
01:53:44,787 --> 01:53:51,787
Sleep occupies nearly a third of our lives, but many of us give surprisingly little attention and care to it.

291
01:53:51,787 --> 01:53:55,787
This neglect is often the result of a major misunderstanding.

292
01:53:55,787 --> 01:54:01,787
Sleep isn't lost time, or just a way to rest when all our important work is done.

293
01:54:01,787 --> 01:54:07,787
Instead, it's a critical function, during which your body balances and regulates its vital systems.

294
01:54:08,015 --> 01:54:14,216
Effecting respiration and regulating everything from circulation to growth and immune response.

295
01:54:14,996 --> 01:54:19,935
That's great, but you can worry about all those things after this test, right?

296
01:54:19,935 --> 01:54:21,615
Well, not so fast.

297
01:54:21,615 --> 01:54:25,095
It turns out that sleep is also crucial for your brain,

298
01:54:25,095 --> 01:54:30,235
with a fifth of your body's circulatory blood being channeled to it as you drift off.

299
01:54:30,235 --> 01:54:35,755
And what goes on in your brain while you sleep is an intensely active period of restructuring

300
01:54:35,755 --> 01:54:38,028
that's crucial for how our memory works.

301
01:54:38,420 --> 01:54:43,420
At first glance, our ability to remember things doesn't seem very impressive at all.

302
01:54:43,420 --> 01:54:52,420
Nineteenth century psychologist Herman Ebbinghaus demonstrated that we normally forget 40% of new material within the first 20 minutes,

303
01:54:52,420 --> 01:54:55,420
a phenomenon known as the forgetting curve.

304
01:54:56,148 --> 01:55:08,067
But this loss can be prevented through memory consolidation, the process by which information is moved from our fleeting short-term memory to our more durable long-term memory.

305
01:55:08,067 --> 01:55:14,528
This consolidation occurs with the help of a major part of the brain, known as the hippocampus.

306
01:55:14,528 --> 01:55:23,692
Its role in long-term memory formation was demonstrated in the 1950s by Brenda Milner in her research with a patient known as H.M.

307
01:55:23,828 --> 01:55:41,280
After having his hippocampus removed, HM's ability to form new short-term memories was damaged, but he was able to learn physical tasks through repetition. Due to the removal of his hippocampus, HM's ability to form long-term memories was also damaged.

308
01:55:41,279 --> 01:55:59,979
What this case revealed, among other things, was that the hippocampus was specifically involved in the consolidation of long-term declarative memory, such as the facts and concepts you need to remember for that test, rather than procedural memory, such as the finger movements you need to master for that recital.

309
01:56:00,627 --> 01:56:09,627
Milner's findings, along with work by Eric Candle in the 90s, have given us our current model of how this consolidation process works.

310
01:56:09,627 --> 01:56:15,627
Sensory data is initially transcribed and temporarily recorded in the neurons as short-term memory.

311
01:56:15,627 --> 01:56:21,627
From there, it travels to the hippocampus, which strengthens and enhances the neurons in that cortical area.

312
01:56:21,627 --> 01:56:28,627
Thanks to the phenomenon of neuroplasticity, new synaptic buds are formed, allowing new connections

313
01:56:28,627 --> 01:56:30,627
This is a way to remember some things and not others.

314
01:56:30,627 --> 01:56:35,627
Well, there are a few ways to influence the extent and effectiveness of memory retention.

315
01:56:35,627 --> 01:56:41,627
For example, memories that are formed in times of heightened feeling or even stress will be better recorded due to the hippocampus's link with emotion.

316
01:56:53,619 --> 01:57:00,619
But one of the major factors contributing to memory consolidation is, you guessed it, a good night's sleep.

317
01:57:00,800 --> 01:57:28,300
Sleep is composed of four stages, the deepest of which are known as slow-wave sleep and rapid eye movement. EEG machines monitoring people during these stages have shown electrical impulses moving between the brainstem, hippocampus, thalamus, and cortex, which serve as relay stations of memory formation. And the different stages of sleep have been shown to help consolidate different types of memories.

318
01:57:28,340 --> 01:57:49,840
During the non-REM slow wave sleep, declarative memory is encoded into a temporary store in the anterior part of the hippocampus. Through a continuing dialogue between the cortex and hippocampus, it is then repeatedly reactivated, driving its gradual redistribution to long-term storage in the cortex.

319
01:57:49,887 --> 01:57:57,887
REM sleep, on the other hand, with its similarity to waking brain activity, is associated with the consolidation of procedural memory.

320
01:57:57,887 --> 01:58:07,211
So based on the studies, going to sleep three hours after memorizing your formulas, and one hour after practicing your scales, would be the most ideal.

321
01:58:07,507 --> 01:58:18,007
So hopefully you can see now that skimping on sleep not only harms your long-term health, but actually makes it less likely that you'll retain all that knowledge and practice from the previous night.

322
01:58:18,163 --> 01:58:22,663
All of which just goes to affirm the wisdom of the phrase, sleep on it.

323
01:58:22,663 --> 01:58:28,663
When you think about all the internal restructuring and forming of new connections that occurs while you slumber,

324
01:58:28,663 --> 01:58:36,663
you could even say that proper sleep will have you waking up every morning with a new and improved brain, ready to face the challenges ahead.

